{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.CODATAChain","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.CODATAChain","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.ArchimedeanMassFormula","lean:module:QOU.MassDerivation","lean:module:QOU.KnotRegistry","lean:module:QOU.Calculations","lean:module:QOU.HadronicMass","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.NormNum","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Positivity","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.FieldSimp","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Pow.Real"],"declarations":10,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.CODATAChain.end_to_end_nucleon_ratio","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.CODATAChain.end_to_end_nucleon_ratio","kind":"theorem","line":193,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/CODATAChain.lean#L193-L196","doc":"The nucleon mass ratio m_n/m_p matches CODATA to 6 decimal places. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.CODATAChain.end_to_end_splitting","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.CODATAChain.end_to_end_splitting","kind":"theorem","line":188,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/CODATAChain.lean#L188-L191","doc":"The proton–neutron mass splitting is exactly 1.29333251 MeV. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.CODATAChain.end_to_end","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.CODATAChain.end_to_end","kind":"theorem","line":165,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/CODATAChain.lean#L165-L186","doc":"**End-to-end theorem**: the full chain from QOU theory to CODATA.\n\nAssuming the `ArchimedeanMassFormula` hypotheses (baseline = volume,\nexceptional = dilogarithm) and the knot-particle ansatz (muon = 4₁),\nthe QOU framework reproduces:\n\n1. Proton mass:  938.272 089 43 MeV (exact match)\n2. Neutron mass: 939.565 421 94 MeV (exact match)\n3. Deuteron mass: within 0.567 keV of CODATA\n4. Binding energy: within 0.567 keV of CODATA\n\nThe substrate parameter q ≈ 1.1097 is derived (not assumed),\nwith `|q − 1.1097| < 0.001` proved by interval arithmetic. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.CODATAChain.substrate_from_archimedean_formula","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.CODATAChain.substrate_from_archimedean_formula","kind":"theorem","line":145,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/CODATAChain.lean#L145-L158","doc":"**Full chain theorem**: Given an `ArchimedeanMassFormula` for\nthe muon (knot volume = Vol(4₁)), and the CODATA mass ratio as\ninput, the substrate parameter is determined to be q ≈ 1.1097.\n\nThis repackages `Calculations.full_derivation_chain` to show\nit is a consequence of the `ArchimedeanMassFormula`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.CODATAChain.h_q_eq_quantum_planck","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.CODATAChain.h_q_eq_quantum_planck","kind":"theorem","line":139,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/CODATAChain.lean#L139-L143","doc":"The `ArchimedeanMassFormula` h_q definition is consistent with\n`MassDerivation.quantum_planck`, via the `h_q_def` constraint. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.CODATAChain.mass_ratio_reproduces_CODATA","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.CODATAChain.mass_ratio_reproduces_CODATA","kind":"theorem","line":118,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/CODATAChain.lean#L118-L131","doc":"When the ansatz volume is Vol(4₁) and q matches the derived substrate\nparameter, the mass ratio equals the experimental muon–electron ratio\n(within the tolerance of the q-derivation).\n\nSpecifically: if `amf.h_q = quantum_planck q` and\n`experimental_mu_e_ratio = vol_figure_eight / (quantum_planck q)²`,\nthen the formula reproduces CODATA. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.CODATAChain.mass_ratio_from_ansatz","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.CODATAChain.mass_ratio_from_ansatz","kind":"theorem","line":104,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/CODATAChain.lean#L104-L116","doc":"**Mass ratio theorem (muon/electron).**\n\nGiven an `ArchimedeanMassFormula` whose knot volume equals\n`Vol(4₁)` (the figure-eight knot — the muon ansatz), the\nmass ratio `m_μ / m_e` equals `Vol(4₁) / ℏ_q²`.\n\nThe denominator `m_e` is the mass of the electron (unknot,\nwith mass set to 1 in the ratio). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.CODATAChain.toTopologicalMassFormula_vol","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.CODATAChain.toTopologicalMassFormula_vol","kind":"theorem","line":92,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/CODATAChain.lean#L92-L96","doc":"The constructed formula uses the knot volume as its volume parameter. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.CODATAChain.toTopologicalMassFormula_mass","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.CODATAChain.toTopologicalMassFormula_mass","kind":"theorem","line":86,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/CODATAChain.lean#L86-L90","doc":"The constructed `TopologicalMassFormula` recovers the original mass. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.CODATAChain.toTopologicalMassFormula","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.CODATAChain.toTopologicalMassFormula","kind":"def","line":72,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/CODATAChain.lean#L72-L84","doc":"Construct a `TopologicalMassFormula` from any `ArchimedeanMassFormula`.\n\nThis is the key bridge: the categorical decomposition theorem\n(with its two evaluation hypotheses) produces the closed-form\nformula that the numerical chain requires. ","signatureChunk":0}]}